{
	title: 'Steering and a visible inbox',
	spec: 'Ordinary sending while a turn runs durably enters the session inbox (in history) and immediately interrupts its active round (task 8p): steering. The host preserves partial output and tool results, suppresses undispatched calls, and requests again with every waiting message together, as one user message, once cancelled work settles. Escape/close wins over that restart. Retrying and login-blocked rounds take the same interrupt-and-re-request path. Alt-Enter queues instead: the message runs after the turn ends, never interrupting. Advisory sends do not interrupt; send steer:true does. The inbox is part of the snapshot and every client shows it all the time, as the tail of the transcript: right after the last item, never across the full-mode padding of the terminal; each waiting message uses the same normal prompt-card renderer and shared header as its delivered form, never a separate kind > text format. Human steering messages say You (steering), both while waiting and after delivery; other senders retain their identity plus the steering or advisory kind. Steering provenance belongs to each delivered text block, not the entire record: delivering steering alongside a fresh ordinary prompt must not label that fresh prompt steering. Live events, reconnects and loaded earlier history agree; older delivered records recover per-text steering from their inbox IDs when those origins are loaded. The label is the kind and sender only, never why the session stalls (that is the status line). A prompt still being sent is drawn after it as a normal prompt card, and the host copy (the inbox or prompt event carrying the id it was sent with) replaces it in that same event, not at the ack after it, so no frame shows it twice; queued messages always either run or stay visibly waiting with a reason (paused, blocked), never hidden behind a command; a host that takes over starts the oldest queued message on any idle session still holding some (the old host died between a turn end and the next queued prompt). Escape pauses; queued messages run when the user continues. See tasks/j1/states.md (Talking while it works). Check that repeated steering keeps the prompt cache warm (cacheRead in usage). On the web the inbox belongs inside the scrollable transcript, never the fixed composer: long waiting messages cannot shrink the reading area or strand scrolling above an open phone keyboard. A waiting row keeps the submit ID through the optimistic and inbox stages, shows its kind/sender, links attachments normally, and replaces the still-unacknowledged optimistic row immediately without duplication. When a queued message is taken to start its turn, its delivered prompt header visibly says from queue rather than becoming an indistinguishable normal user message. Queue origin is durable on the delivered record and carried in live events, so terminal/web and live/reloaded scrollback agree; a message sent with Queue while idle is not marked as popped because it never waited. The original submit ID accompanies queue delivery to retain its optimistic/waiting row identity. For older records, queue origin can be recovered from delivered inbox IDs when their inbox records are in loaded history.',
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		"Prompt-cache warmth after a steer (cacheRead in usage) is checked only in offline tests. A live check was not possible on 27 Sep: this home's copied credentials were invalid_grant. Check it live once /login gives the home its own login.",
		"Durable form: an `inbox` record { id, text, queue? } per message sent while busy (id = the client's command id, else a random UUID, so host.submitted recognises a resend to a new host), and a `user` prompt record with `inbox: [ids]` delivers them; its text blocks are the messages, then any new prompt. inbox.pending(records) (src/common/inbox.ts) is the inbox. replay skips inbox records and joins a prompt's texts with blank lines into one block after the [HH:MM] line.",
		'states.step keeps a busy state on submit: steering is not a state. host.submit: busy, or `queue` on a paused or failed session, appends to the inbox; `queue` on idle just submits; a submit that starts a turn delivers waiting steering messages first (oldest first, one prompt: a `prompt` event, then a turn-start without prompt).',
		'host.runTurn calls host.steer before every request (first round, after tool results, retries), and a round that finished without tool calls loops once more if steering waits. After a completed turn host.next runs the oldest queued message as a turn of its own (one at a time). Paused and error ends leave the inbox waiting; continue delivers the steering, completion then runs the queue.',
		"Protocol: submit.queue (boolean), Snapshot.inbox (optional so an older host still works), events `inbox` (the whole list) and `prompt` (texts now in history; transcript.fold settles the live round's blocks before them, like tool-results). Transcript.inbox is always an array.",
		'Clients: states.enter(id, state, text, queue) never refuses text now. Terminal: Alt-Enter (ESC CR or CSI-u alt) is a submit action with queue; frame.build draws each waiting message above the notice as `inbox.label(state, item): text`. Web: e.altKey on Enter queues; view.inbox() fills a #inbox div in the footer.',
		"Prompt cache: host tests assert each steered request's messages start with the previous request's messages (anthropic caches the whole prefix at the last block), which is what keeps cacheRead up. Not checked live against the API: copying credentials into a test home rotates the refresh token (see tf notes).",
		'Recovery of a queue left on an idle session: host.recover() reads history.tail(id) (the whole lines of the last 64 KiB) and, when the last record is a completed turn end and the tail holds a queued inbox record or no prompt at all, reads the history and calls host.next. A queued message older than both the tail and the last prompt in it is missed; reading every history at startup was judged too slow.',
		'Round-boundary steering (notes above) was replaced by immediate interruption in task 8p; the delivery-before-each-request code still carries the messages.',
		"Waiting messages are prompt items built by transcript.waitingItem, so terminal frame and web Card use the normal header (titles.who); the old '(steering) > text' tail was removed. inbox.provenance decides steering once (not queue, not advisory); prompts.deliver stamps it on each text block, and fromSnapshot recovers it for older records from loaded inbox records."
